\begin{align*}
n &\equiv 0 \pmod{4}, \\
n &\equiv 2 \pmod{3}, \\
n &\equiv 5 \pmod{11}.
\end{align*}

We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to solve this system of congruences. First, we solve the first two congruences:

\begin{align*}
n &\equiv 0 \pmod{4}, \\
n &\equiv 2 \pmod{3}.
\end{align*}

This means that $n$ is a multiple of $12$ plus $2$. We can write $n = 12k + 2$ for some integer $k$. Now we substitute this into the third congruence:

\begin{align*}
12k + 2 &\equiv 5 \pmod{11}, \\
12k &\equiv 3 \pmod{11}.
\end{align*}

We can simplify this congruence by dividing both sides by $3$:

\begin{align*}
4k &\equiv 1 \pmod{11}.
\end{align*}

We can solve this congruence by finding the multiplicative inverse of $4$ modulo $11$. Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, we find that $4 \cdot 9 \equiv 1 \pmod{11}$, so the inverse is $9$. Therefore, we can multiply both sides of the congruence by $9$:

\begin{align*}
k &\equiv 9 \cdot 9 \pmod{11}, \\
k &\equiv 81 \pmod{11}, \\
k &\equiv 3 \pmod{11}.
\end{align*}

This means that $k = 11m + 3$ for some integer $m$. Now we substitute this back into the expression for $n$:

\begin{align*}
n &= 12(11m + 3) + 2, \\
n &= 132m + 38.
\end{align*}

So the number of soldiers in the regiment is $\boxed{170}$.
